# hole_member_non_struct_receiver — a method called on something that is not a struct
# is not checked: `n.foo()` for `mut n: int` is accepted by `check`, and the refusal
# arrives from the back end (`vela: panic: this struct has no method of that name`).
#
# `099f6fc` refuses `Pt has no method 'dotq'` when the receiver *is* a struct and the
# method is not; this row is the other half — there is no struct at all to look a
# method up in, and the checker does not say so.
#
# The interpreter's answer is its own sentence (`only a struct has methods`, exit 2),
# so this is also a case where the two front ends word the same mistake differently.
#
# SPEC.md §3 (types) and §4 (methods are struct members).  `violation` row: it reports
# CLOSED once the checker refuses it itself.

def main() -> None {
    mut n: int = 1
    print(n.foo())
}
